you to organize and monitor

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.610
those environments so that
you don't have runaway costs

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.270
and that you really spend
what you need to in order

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:12.350
to manage a business.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, for them,
it's a huge change in thinking.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.730
But I know that they also
see the benefit of the cloud

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.520
technologies with services
like Serverless that

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.740
let their developers
focus on the code and not

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.612
the infrastructure.

00:02:22.612 --> 00:02:25.070
And then operationally, they
can scale those resources down

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:25.910
to zero.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.070
So how has this
change in thinking

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.728
really influenced billing and
cost management for people?

00:02:30.728 --> 00:02:33.020
MATT LEONARD: From pure
development standpoint, I mean,

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.780
Cloud has opened up all
sorts of new opportunities

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.790
to solve new and
interesting problems,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.400
but you can also take
your legacy applications

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.123
and move them to the cloud
and really rightsize them.

00:02:43.123 --> 00:02:44.540
Whereas before,
you might have had

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.560
to deploy an extra large
database for your peak

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.710
workload, now you can
scale up dynamically,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:53.570
as you need to, as
you hit that peak load

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.970
and only pay for what
you need in order

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.050
to manage your environment.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.200
And what we're
seeing over time is

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.270
that as services
are rearchitected,

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.000
that they start to take
advantage of cloud native

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.570
services like Kubernetes that
allow you to automatically

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.330
or dynamically scale your
service to exactly what you

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.450
need to consume.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.490
STEPHANIE WONG: So let's
talk about some examples

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.980
of that, of ways to
optimize costs in the cloud.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.680
I've heard of things like
rightsizing machines,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.870
preemptible machines,
committed use discounts,

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.123
sustained use discounts.

00:03:22.123 --> 00:03:23.540
So can you explain
the differences

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:24.980
in when you would
use each of those?

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.772
MATT LEONARD: Sure.

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.470
Yeah, there's really
two big buckets

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.200
of how you would do cost
optimization with Cloud.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.210
One is really about
resource optimization,

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.963
and the other one is
pricing optimization.

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:35.630
So in the case of
resource optimization,

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.370
you have a few different
options that we

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.710
support within Google Cloud
or across your environment.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.260
One is effectively identifying
underutilized resources.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.632
So that can be
through Rightsizing.

00:03:48.632 --> 00:03:50.090
So if you have a
four-core machine,

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.100
and you really need only one
core at your peak capacity.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.200
You can downsize that
to a one-core machine,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.340
spend less money.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.930
Idle instances or idle resources
that are essentially abandoned,

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.240
we'll identify them so that
you can shut them down.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.740
Or even resource
scheduling-- if you

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.140
have a cyclical
pattern of usage,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:09.145
we can help you automate the
lifecycle of that resource.

00:04:09.145 --> 00:04:11.020
So that's what we call
resource optimization.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.687
And then the second
big bucket is really

00:04:12.687 --> 00:04:15.440
pricing optimization-- how can
I do the same amount of work

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:16.350
but for less money?

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.980
And that's when things like
sustained use discounts, which

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:21.740
automatically calculate
the lowest possible cost

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.500
and give you a discount for
continually running machines.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.390
Or committed use
discounts-- if you

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.010
plan to have a steady state
over a long period of time,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.675
you can make a
commitment which nets

00:04:30.675 --> 00:04:33.650
you a large discount in terms
of the day-to-day running

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.825
of that machine.

00:04:34.825 --> 00:04:36.200
And we have more
models like that

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.867
that allow you,
essentially, to buy down

00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:40.100
the cost of that machine,
relative to the lifecycle

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:40.850
of it.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:41.210
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.650
So those are cost optimization.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.570
But how do you actually
organize your business

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.030
in the cloud to take
advantage of those things?

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.220
MATT LEONARD: So that's
a great point, actually.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.070
About 50% of all the inbound
calls we get for help

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.840
is, how do we even get started?

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.240
How do I organize my
business on Google Cloud?

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.280
You can't do cost
optimization if you're not

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.220
already organized.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.850
And so where we've seen
customers struggle in the past

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.790
is that they've organically
grown on the cloud

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.380
without really thinking about
their business structure.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.680
And so we offer a
variety of mechanisms

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.440
to help you segregate your
business into business units.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.920
And so we have what we call
the resource hierarchy,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.740
which is orgs, folders,
projects, and then ultimately

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.110
user labels, which are
attached to resources.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.800
You can use any or a
combination of these in order

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.150
to organize your costs.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.538
Once you have workloads
organized into discrete units,

00:05:27.538 --> 00:05:29.080
at that point, you
can start to think

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.840
about optimizing either
as an individual workload

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.180
or across all of your
workloads in totality.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:34.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Does
it make a difference

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.127
depending on the structure
of your business, though?

00:05:37.127 --> 00:05:38.740
MATT LEONARD:
Absolutely, actually.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.590
We see customers
using and organizing

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.340
their business differently
on Google Cloud

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:44.740
depending on what's
most important to them

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:45.920
and their workloads.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.920
We have some customers
who it's really important

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.710
that they have autonomous
control of the business units.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.650
And so they might completely
segregate a set of resources

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.590
from another set of resources
from a cost standpoint

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:01.120
in order to delegate control
financially for that workload.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.910
We see other customers who
need all of their workloads

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.853
to coexist within the same
organizational bucket.

00:06:06.853 --> 00:06:09.520
And in those cases, they tend to
use user labels as the strategy

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:10.670
for organizing their costs.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.910
But in those particular
cases, it's really important

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.410
that you've thought
about like, what

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.540
is the hierarchy of my
labels on those resources?

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.617
Because again, your organization
isn't a single layer.

00:06:19.617 --> 00:06:21.700
And so, therefore, your
cost organization strategy

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.280
should also not
be a single layer.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.530
So think about that first
before you do anything else.

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:27.947
STEPHANIE WONG:
So like an example

00:06:27.947 --> 00:06:30.400
could be, not just dev and
test environments, but maybe

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.283
organized by project or teams
for HR, finance, et cetera.

00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:35.950
MATT LEONARD: Absolutely,
or potentially

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.150
organized by line
of business, right?

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:39.340
Take Google, for example--

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.780
we're not just Google Cloud.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.670
We consist of YouTube and Ads
and many other businesses.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:45.580
And so as we started to
organize our business

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.380
running on top of
Google Cloud, we

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.640
were put in operating divisions.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.643
And then we would potentially
segregate between production

00:06:52.643 --> 00:06:54.310
and development
environments within that

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:56.290
or within different
teams, depending

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:57.820
on who's managing the cost.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.380
STEPHANIE WONG: Now that you
know where the costs are coming

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:01.690
from in this case, how do
you actually get visibility

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:02.898
into controlling those costs?

00:07:02.898 --> 00:07:05.770
Because I can imagine with
so many teams, at Google too,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.080
you might have mushrooming
costs that are inadvertent,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.600
you spin up a VM and it just
spirals in terms of costs.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:12.850
How do we control that?

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.230
MATT LEONARD: Yeah, it's
a challenging problem.

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:16.905
I would say the first thing that
you want to do is you probably,

00:07:16.905 --> 00:07:18.520
and it depends on
your organization,

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.337
but delegating control to
the owners of the workloads

00:07:21.337 --> 00:07:22.670
is a really good place to start.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:25.330
And within Google Cloud, within
our cost management tooling,

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:26.890
we have a bunch
of different tools

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:30.770
that allow you to delegate that
control through our reporting

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:31.270
frameworks.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.800
And you can delegate at any
level of your organization.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.520
But we have reporting
that gives you

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.225
visibility through time
series visualizations

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:41.350
that you can break down by
environment, by product,

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:42.767
by SKU, essentially
however you've

00:07:42.767 --> 00:07:43.880
organized your workloads.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.503
We also have detailed
billing exports

00:07:45.503 --> 00:07:47.170
that allow you to get
access to the data

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:49.400
and build your own dashboards
so that you can view the data.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.190
And lastly, we have financial
governance controls.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.540
And I think that's
really important

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:53.915
and probably where
you were going

00:07:53.915 --> 00:07:56.440
with this is, how do I build
that financial governance

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:58.810
into my plan for Cloud?

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.450
So within that, we have
tooling for budgets,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.570
which allow you to monitor
costs over time per workload

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:06.930
or across your organization.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.520
Or we have quota, which
allows you to essentially set

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.370
a limit for how many resources
an individual workload

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.835
can consume or what a
division can consume.

00:08:14.835 --> 00:08:16.210
And essentially
what that does is

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:18.970
it sets the upper limit or the
upper threshold for resource

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:19.710
consumption.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:20.800
STEPHANIE WONG: I could
see visibility still

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:21.717
being a big challenge.

00:08:21.717 --> 00:08:24.400
With hundreds of teams, you have
per second, per minute billing

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.580
costs that pop up.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.370
So how do you identify
root causes and trends?

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.370
MATT LEONARD: Identifying
the root cause of cost

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:31.607
can be challenging.

00:08:31.607 --> 00:08:33.940
Part of what we offer you in
the cost management tooling

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.357
is the ability to
drill into cost.

00:08:35.357 --> 00:08:38.694
To starting at the org level,
at the highest level, like where

00:08:38.694 --> 00:08:41.580
is my cost coming from, and
am I monitoring for spikes?

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.750
Our tooling actually allows
you to drill all the way

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:47.090
down to individual skews by
workload, by environment,

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.050
depending on how you've
organized your costs.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.690
And we provide
hourly level data.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.800
Now, it's not per
second because honestly,

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:57.462
trying to consume that amount
of data and react at that level,

00:08:57.462 --> 00:08:59.170
it's not really
something a human can do.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:01.832
And because of that, we offer
these financial governing tools

00:09:01.832 --> 00:09:03.290
that actually allow
you to monitor.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:04.582
So they monitor for conditions.

00:09:04.582 --> 00:09:07.140
And so when you set a
budget for an environment,

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.890
it's not just looking
at your actual cost.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:10.765
It's looking at the
forecast of cost as well.

00:09:10.765 --> 00:09:12.922
And as huge costs
change over time,

00:09:12.922 --> 00:09:14.380
you can get real-time
notifications

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.200
that say, hey, Stephanie,
your workload is changing.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.860
You should probably
come take a look,

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.693
see if something changed
in your environment

00:09:20.693 --> 00:09:23.293
that you want to maybe make
a change in response to.

00:09:23.293 --> 00:09:25.210
STEPHANIE WONG: I imagine
all these activities

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:26.710
could be pretty
challenging to keep

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.190
track of at enterprise scale.

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:30.520
So for example, if
I had a team where

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.423
we were running
[INAUDIBLE] machine,

00:09:32.423 --> 00:09:34.840
and then we had a database
team and analytics team running

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:38.245
queries against 8 terabytes of
data, another team with request

00:09:38.245 --> 00:09:41.080
to ML APIs, how do you
really optimize for cost

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.250
in all those scenarios?

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:44.583
MATT LEONARD: Yeah, it can
be really challenging, right?

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:48.340
Like, if you've got a really
complex discounting mechanism,

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:50.020
like committed use
discount, which

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.110
operates on
microsecond increments,

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.562
just the sheer thought of
trying to rationalize that data

00:09:55.562 --> 00:09:57.520
and think about like,
what kind of a commitment

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.220
do I want to make, I'm
signing my organization up,

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.660
can be very complex.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:03.970
That's why we have what
we call recommendations,

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:07.345
where we want to service to
you the correct recommendation

00:10:07.345 --> 00:10:08.470
for committed use discount.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:11.063
So it takes the
guesswork out of it.

00:10:11.063 --> 00:10:12.980
We have the tools that
allow you to drill down

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:15.440
to that level of visibility
with accurate start-stop times

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.880
broken down into hourly chunks.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:18.002
But let's make it easy.

00:10:18.002 --> 00:10:19.460
Let's give you the
tools that allow

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.920
you to make those decisions
for your organization

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:23.670
without having to
have a PhD in billing.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.170
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's pretty funny.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.170
That's the first time
I've heard that, actually.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:29.930
MATT LEONARD: Yeah, it's
one of those things that--

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:32.595
Cloud Billing's often accused
of being far too complex.

00:10:32.595 --> 00:10:34.220
And one of the things
that we've really

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:35.928
been driving at Google
Cloud is how do we

00:10:35.928 --> 00:10:40.437
simplify the experience overall
so that users can understand

00:10:40.437 --> 00:10:42.270
the discounting mechanisms
that we're using,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.890
and they don't need to have
this deep understanding of how

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:47.498
the mechanics work
under the hood.

00:10:47.498 --> 00:10:49.790
STEPHANIE WONG: What about
access controls and policies

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:52.280
and rolling that down
throughout your org hierarchy?

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.860
Like, for example, if I had
a database team or analytics

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.700
team, how do I prevent them from
spinning up hundreds of nodes

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.560
for a Spanner database?

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:02.527
MATT LEONARD: Yeah,
wow, that sounds scary.

00:11:02.527 --> 00:11:04.610
So quota is the first way
that you do that, right?

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.370
So by setting a quota limit,
you can set the resource limit

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:08.240
for a given team.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:09.740
How much can they consume?

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:11.750
You also use identity
and access management

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.318
in order to ensure
least privilege

00:11:13.318 --> 00:11:14.360
within your organization.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.930
And that's something that you
should be actively managing.

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:20.030
And then from a pure
cost standpoint,

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.370
we allow access
to workload owners

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.180
to view the cost data so they
can manage it themselves.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.105
But we also provide
aggregate views

00:11:28.105 --> 00:11:29.480
of that at the
organization level

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:32.330
so that your billing
administrators or your org

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.247
admins can view the costs
on an ongoing basis.

00:11:34.247 --> 00:11:35.622
STEPHANIE WONG:
It seems like you

00:11:35.622 --> 00:11:37.610
have to pull in the right
people in many ways--

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:40.152
the development teams and the
actual billing management teams

00:11:40.152 --> 00:11:43.067
as well-- and kind of control
it from various perspectives.

00:11:43.067 --> 00:11:44.900
MATT LEONARD: It's
definitely a team effort.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:46.700
And it helps if you
start with a policy

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.650
in mind of how you want
to organize and govern

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:50.153
your business.

00:11:50.153 --> 00:11:51.320
And then you can apply that.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:52.445
You can automate it, right?

00:11:52.445 --> 00:11:55.690
You can use Terraform in
order to create policy

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:58.310
in the form of budget,
in the form of quota,

00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.780
and identity and
access management

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.200
so that when your teams go to
do development that they have

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.730
the right level of control.

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.120
STEPHANIE WONG: I want to talk
a little bit about chargebacks

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.220
because I know
this can be pretty

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:09.770
common with large organizations
and lots of teams spinning up

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:10.610
resources.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.730
So do you see it being
common to do chargebacks

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:15.230
or separate invoicing?

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.855
MATT LEONARD: So that's going to
really depend on your business

00:12:17.855 --> 00:12:19.360
and what you're optimizing for.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.050
We've got very
large customers who

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:23.210
have a single billing
account and single invoice

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:24.560
for their entire business.

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.690
They essentially use the
organizational constructs

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:28.983
we talked about earlier
of folders and projects

00:12:28.983 --> 00:12:30.650
for each of the
different business units

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:32.525
And they use chargeback
through the reporting

00:12:32.525 --> 00:12:34.880
and detailed billing exports
that we provide in order

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.040
to create the visibility
and chargeback that they

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:38.400
need for their business.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:40.850
We have other customers
who basically are

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:42.080
managing via purchase order.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.910
And because of that, they need
separate billing accounts,

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:45.620
which means separate invoices.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:48.320
And that allows them
the autonomy within each

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.227
of those business units to--

00:12:50.227 --> 00:12:52.560
instead of using chargeback,
to get individual invoices.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.260
Now, even in those
customers, there's

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.450
still an element of
chargeback, right?

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:59.960
That individual invoice might
represent multiple operating

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.700
entities within the team, might
be different applications,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.420
it might be different
teams, actually,

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:06.710
within the structure of that.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:09.340
And the tooling, the
reporting, the exports, they

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:11.090
allow you to drill
down to that fine grain

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.173
level within your invoice.

00:13:12.173 --> 00:13:13.590
STEPHANIE WONG:
So we have support

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:14.950
for either of those use cases.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:15.620
MATT LEONARD: Absolutely.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:16.880
We want to give
you the flexibility

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:19.088
to build your business on
top of Google Cloud the way

00:13:19.088 --> 00:13:20.510
that you need it to operate.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:21.650
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, so
how about contracting?

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:23.360
Because I know this is very
important for a company

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.040
that works with a cloud vendor.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.025
How do we help provide
them with complex pricing?

00:13:29.025 --> 00:13:31.025
MATT LEONARD: So Google
Cloud provides a variety

00:13:31.025 --> 00:13:32.590
of mechanisms for discounting.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.940
I'll split it into what
we call custom contracting

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:36.920
and what we call
self-service discounting.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:39.440
So things like committed
use discounts and BigQuery

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.500
flat-rates, these are things
that customers can negotiate

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:43.580
effectively on their own.

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.288
If you come into our
console experiences,

00:13:45.288 --> 00:13:47.510
you can actually think
about your environment

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.400
and purchase those without the
assistance of an account team.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.310
Then we have what we
call custom contracting.

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:56.510
Within a custom contracting,
we can negotiate discounts

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.090
for specific workloads,
specific products.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.030
And you work for an account
team in order to finalize that.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:03.590
And then those
discounts will flow

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:05.923
through all of your billing
data into all of your views,

00:14:05.923 --> 00:14:08.210
so that you can
understand and rationalize

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:11.120
the impact of your contract
relative to the cost of Cloud.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.370
STEPHANIE WONG: So we just
covered a lot, but what can

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.892
people do to get value out
of cost optimizations today?

00:14:15.892 --> 00:14:17.100
MATT LEONARD: Great question.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:19.670
I would recommend that you start
by visiting the Google Cloud

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.450
cost management page, which
has a repository of all

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:28.130
the content, including white
papers, links to our videos

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:30.840
from Next Talks and Beyond
the Bill video series

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.980
that explain basic concepts
for you around cost management.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:35.960
You can check out
the playlist below.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:36.380
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:38.030
Well, thank you so much
for being on the show.

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:38.660
MATT LEONARD: Thank you.

